{"name":"Dark Elf","hex":"#3b3f42","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dark elf","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the race of Dǫkkálfar or Dark Alfs, creatures who live underground.","A member of a race of elves that is evil, has a dark (often greyish or bluish) skin and/or lives in dark places."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dark_elf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dökkálfar and Ljósálfar","blocks":[{"text":"In Norse mythology, Dökkálfar and Ljósálfar are two contrasting types of elves; the dark elves dwell within the earth and have a dark complexion, while the light elves live in Álfheimr, and are \"fairer than the sun to look at\". The Ljósálfar and the Dökkálfar are attested in the Prose Edda, written in the 13th century by Snorri Sturluson, and in the late Old Norse poem Hrafnagaldr Óðins. Scholars have produced theories about the origin and implications of the dualistic concept."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/%C3%84lvalek.jpg/330px-%C3%84lvalek.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%B6kk%C3%A1lfar_and_Lj%C3%B3s%C3%A1lfar"},{"title":"Svartálfar","blocks":[{"text":"In Norse cosmology, svartálfar, also called myrkálfar, are beings who dwell in Svartálfheim. Both the svartálfar and Svartálfaheimr are only attested in the Prose Edda, written in the 13th century by Snorri Sturluson. Scholars have noted that the svartálfar appear to be synonymous with the dwarfs and potentially also the dökkálfar. As dwarfs, the home of the svartálfar could possibly be another description for Niðavellir."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Svart%C3%A1lfar"},{"title":"Elf","blocks":[{"text":"An elf is a type of humanoid supernatural being in Germanic folklore. Elves appear especially in North Germanic mythology, being mentioned in the Icelandic Poetic Edda and the Prose Edda."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/%C3%84ngs%C3%A4lvor_-_Nils_Blomm%C3%A9r_1850.jpg/330px-%C3%84ngs%C3%A4lvor_-_Nils_Blomm%C3%A9r_1850.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elf"},{"title":"Darkness","blocks":[{"text":"Darkness is the condition resulting from a lack of illumination, or an absence of visible light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Creation_of_Light.png/330px-Creation_of_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darkness"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Phonological_development_of_the_word_elf_in_English.png/500px-Phonological_development_of_the_word_elf_in_English.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Phonological_development_of_the_word_elf_in_English.png/1280px-Phonological_development_of_the_word_elf_in_English.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Phonological_development_of_the_word_elf_in_English.png/500px-Phonological_development_of_the_word_elf_in_English.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A chart showing how the sound of the word elf has changed in the history of English[2][3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhonological_development_of_the_word_elf_in_English.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Alden_Valley_-_geograph.org.uk_-_417197.jpg/500px-Alden_Valley_-_geograph.org.uk_-_417197.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Alden_Valley_-_geograph.org.uk_-_417197.jpg/500px-Alden_Valley_-_geograph.org.uk_-_417197.jpg?utm_source